= CHAPTER ONE

The sea was not water, but a vast, undulating sheet of cold mercury under the October moon. Elias stood at the rail of the *Vesper*, his hands numbed by the salt-spray, watching the lights of the harbor fade into a series of flickering needles. He had left everything behind—the clocks, the ledgers, the stifling silence of his father's house—and yet, the silence of the ocean felt heavier still.

It was a modern silence, he thought. Not the absence of sound, but the presence of an immense, uncaring weight.

#verse[
  "The tide will rise, the tide will fall, \
  The twilight darkens, the curlew calls; \
  Along the sea-sands damp and brown \
  The traveler hastens toward the town."
  — Longfellow
]

He turned away from the rail. The deck below was a hive of muffled activity, the creak of wood and the snap of canvas providing a rhythmic counterpoint to his thoughts. He needed to find the Captain, but the companionway seemed to stretch into an infinite, shadowy tunnel.

#scene-break()

Inside the cabin, the air was thick with the scent of tobacco and old paper. Captain Thorne was hunched over a chart, a brass divider in his hand. He did not look up when Elias entered, but the tip of his cigar flared a brilliant, angry orange.

"We aren't in the channel yet," Thorne said, his voice a gravelly rasp. "Go back to your bunk, Mr. Vane. The night is long, and the charts are lies."

"I 
cannot sleep," Elias replied, his voice steadier than he felt. "The charts might be lies, but the stars are staying still."

Thorne finally looked up, his eyes two dark pits in a weathered face. "The stars don't care where we go. That’s why sailors love them."
